	annote = {Paper Review:
{\textgreater} *** Relevance and timeliness: Rate the importance and timeliness of the topic addressed in the paper within its area of research.
Average (2)
{\textgreater} *** Technical content and scientific rigour: Rate the technical content of the paper (e.g.: completeness of the analysis or simulation study, thoroughness of the treatise, accuracy of the models, etc.), its soundness and scientific rigour.
Valid work but limited contribution. (3)
{\textgreater} *** Novelty and originality: Rate the novelty and originality of the ideas or results presented in the paper.
Significant original work and novel results. (4)
{\textgreater} *** Quality of presentation: Rate the paper organization, the clearness of text and figures, the completeness and accuracy of references.
Readable, but revision is needed in some parts. (3)
{\textgreater} *** Overall Recommendation: What is your overall recommendation of this paper?
Accept (4)
{\textgreater} *** Reviewer Familiarity: Please assess your familiarity with the subjectmatter of the paper.
Outside main area of research (1)
{\textgreater} *** Comments: Please justify here your rating of the paper. What are its strengths and weaknesses? Note that these comments will be sent to the authors.
The motivation for the paper is good and the selected deep learning models seem relevant. I am not an expert in the deep learning, so most of my comments will be directed towards network modeling aspects of the paper.
The paper claims that there are no traffic datasets for heterogeneous networks but uses the existing Abilene dataset. There is a significant research novelty in validating the research method proposed here using the well established data sets and traffic models for both homogeneous and heterogeneous network scenarios. Please see CAIDA and similar data sets.
The traffic generation model for self-constructed imbalanced network could be significantly improved. This model could be based on the traffic profiles of wireless users as observed in the standard datasets. Even using the application-specific traffic profiles would be a good validation point.
The use of NS as simulator is a good choice. It would be good to simulate a bigger, more representative network. Any published topology and connection capacities of under-sea networks could be used to ground the simulation to real world scenarios. Similarly, any publicly available wireless network topologies (even if they come from the random scanning of wireless networks as published on Internet) provide some link to real world scenarios.
I like the paper and the solution approach taken in it. However significant improvements are needed in the network model and simulation scenarios for the research work to become significant.

},
	file = {Traffic Prediction Approach based on Deep Learning.pdf:source-pdfs/files/618/Traffic Prediction Approach based on Deep Learning.pdf:application/pdf},
}

