	abstract = {We provide an overview of techniques that can be used to calibrate the models that form the basis of digital twins of cyber-physical systems. Calibration (or parameter estimation) involves finding values for the model parameters that optimise the model’s capabilities, particularly the accuracy of its predictions about the future evolution of the modelled system. We consider techniques to mitigate noise in the data observed from the real system, beginning with a least squares method that is unable to calibrate nonlinear models. A gradient descent method that remedies this is described, as are alternative approaches such asNewton and Gauss-Newton methods. These methods bring the risk of only finding local minima. Genetic algorithms for design space exploration and other heuristic methods are briefly outlined.},
