@misc{noauthor_federated_nodate,
	title = {Federated {Learning} for {Digital} {Twin}-assisted {Speech} {Emotion} {Recognition}},
	annote = {Review

TITLE: 1571230612: Federated Learning for Digital Twin-assisted Speech Emotion Recognition
----------- Degree of Innovation -----------
SCORE: 2 (Incremental (the paper makes modest advancements, refinements, or extensions to current theories, methods, or findings))
----------- Overall Evaluation -----------
SCORE: weak accept
----------- Summary -----------
The paper proposes use of federated learning on digital twins (DTs) of patient emotions built using Speech Emotion Recognition (SER). The aim is to build patient-level DT, followed by aggregation of these DTs within one organizational silos and then a federation across organizations. Well-known SER algorithms have been tested against open-source datasets.
Thank you for the contribution. It has been a pleasure reading the paper. There is clarity in writing and the organization of the paper is good.
----------- Major Strong Points -----------
The paper makes use of DT concepts across multiple scales for a single vertical (medical domain - affective patient care).
The strong points are the use of open-source data sets to aid the reuse and verification. There is a clear use of DT-DT collaboration, DT federation and the use of data layer, DT collaboration interface and DT applications. Thus the architecture while reusing the well-known DT concepts, is clearly innovative for the application domain.
----------- Major Weak Points -----------
There are two major weak points in the paper.
1. The methods used are well-known in the affective care. Perhaps more clarity on the incremental innovation done on the methods would have made clear the contribution on the methods. On the other hand, the current contents of the paper make clear on the the improved methodology.
1. Another major weakness is on the lack of details for federated and transfer learning. There is not sufficient detail both in the proposal and in implementation sections.
----------- Detailed Comments -----------
A subsection (or at least an elaboration) on the kind of information exchange done on DT-DT sidebound interface and northbound interfaces would be nice. In addition, the implementation section contains results on federated learning (figure 4), but the accompanying does not clearly explain the improvement. Perhaps, the last statement is a misunderstanding on my part. So please take it with a bit of salt.

},
	file = {Federated Learning for Digital Twin-assisted Speec.pdf:source-pdfs/files/1900/Federated Learning for Digital Twin-assisted Speec.pdf:application/pdf},
}

